from pathlib import Path
import json
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
path=ROOT/'tools/lesson_interactions.json'
items=json.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
by={str(x['id']):x for x in items}

by['12']['stages']=[
 {'image':'charts/L12-01-before-break.png','alt':'NIFTY 50 daily candles through 2 September 2016. The prior upper zone is highlighted; subsequent candles are hidden.','title':'Stage 1 · Before the break','prompt':'Which earlier highs make this a reasonable upper area to watch? What rule will you use to call a break?','placeholder':'Describe the earlier reactions, state your rule, and name one thing you cannot know yet.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “Price has turned away from this upper area before, so I will use it as a candidate ceiling. For this daily chart, I will count a daily close above the band as a break. Even if that happens, I will not know yet whether price can stay above it.”','selfCheck':'A useful response points to earlier highs, states a clear rule (such as a daily close outside), and leaves the later response open.'},
 {'image':'charts/L12-02-outside-zone.png','alt':'The same NIFTY 50 chart through 8 September 2016, now showing several closes above the highlighted zone.','title':'Stage 2 · Several closes outside','prompt':'Several daily candles have now closed above the band. What does this add to your first reading, and what is still open?','placeholder':'Say which evidence has been added and what would make you reconsider.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “Several daily closes above the band give the breakout attempt more support than the first crossing alone. My rule has been met, but I still need to see whether price continues to hold outside. A return into the old range would weaken the idea.”','selfCheck':'Mention the repeated closes, avoid calling continuation certain, and name a later event that would weaken the reading.'},
 {'image':'charts/L12-03-later-failure.png','alt':'The complete teaching window through 16 September 2016, showing price later returning below the original zone.','title':'Stage 3 · Later evidence','prompt':'Price has now moved back below the original band. What should change in your note—and what should stay in it?','placeholder':'Keep the earlier closes as facts, then describe the new evidence and your revised view.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “The earlier closes above the band were real, but price later closed back below it and moved into the old range. That weakens the idea that the move was holding above resistance. I cannot say from this chart alone why it failed or that a short trade must follow.”','selfCheck':'Keep the earlier evidence, identify the return below the band, update the interpretation, and avoid inventing a cause or a trade.'}
]
by['12']['reveal']='The rule was applied consistently: several daily closes appeared above the chosen band. Later, price returned below it. A fair review keeps both facts and updates the interpretation. The ending was not available at Stage 1.'
by['12']['modelAnswerRubric']=['Points to price behaviour visible at that stage.','Separates the observation from what it may mean.','Names one uncertainty or alternative path.','Says what new evidence could strengthen or weaken the view.']
by['12']['quiz']=[
 {'q':'A candle wicks above resistance but closes back inside the zone. Which description fits?','options':['Price has closed above the chosen zone.','Price traded above the zone but finished back inside.','Price has shown the zone will act as support.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'The high shows where price travelled during the candle; the close shows where it finished. This is an intraday move beyond the zone, not a close outside it.'},
 {'q':'One daily candle closes just above a marked area. What can you say now?','options':['The session finished above the chosen boundary.','The next session is likely to keep rising.','The old resistance is now confirmed support.'],'correct':0,'feedback':'The close is an observation. It tells you where this session ended, not what the next session must do.'},
 {'q':'Two closes are above the zone, but later price closes back inside. How would you review it?','options':['The first two closes should be removed from the record.','The breakout failed, so a short is now required.','The rule was met, then later evidence weakened the move.'],'correct':2,'feedback':'The earlier closes remain valid observations. The later return changes the current reading, but it does not create an automatic opposite-side trade.'},
 {'q':'A chart shows higher volume on both a move that holds and one that fails. What does that suggest?','options':['High volume is useful only on a failed move.','Volume adds context, but does not settle the outcome alone.','The holding move must have had lower volume.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'Volume and price behaviour are two separate clues. Activity can rise in moves that continue and in moves that fail.'},
 {'q':'A fixed 20-point buffer is used on two different instruments. What is the main concern?','options':['The same distance may mean different things in each market.','The buffer guarantees the same quality of signal.','A fixed buffer works only after two closes.'],'correct':0,'feedback':'Instrument scale and normal price movement differ. A fixed distance is a convention to test, not a universal threshold.'},
 {'q':'A five-minute break occurs while price remains inside a broad daily range. Which note is clearest?','options':['The daily trend has definitely changed.','The local break is clear; the wider range still matters.','The daily range no longer needs to be considered.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'Both statements can be true: a local boundary was crossed, while the wider structure remains a range.'},
 {'q':'Which journal note would be most useful to review later?','options':['“Strong breakout. Looks good.”','“Price is doing what it should.”','“Daily close above the zone; I will reconsider after a close back inside.”'],'correct':2,'feedback':'A useful note gives the area, the rule, the observation and a condition that would change your view.'}
]

by['13']['stages']=[
 {'image':'charts/L13-01-before-break.png','alt':'NIFTY 50 daily candles through 14 August 2014, with the candidate former-resistance zone highlighted.','title':'Stage 1 · The old upper area','prompt':'Which earlier reactions support this candidate area? Could another careful reader draw it a little differently?','placeholder':'Point to the earlier highs and say what your band includes.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “Several earlier highs sit near this band, so it is a reasonable candidate ceiling. Another reader might draw the upper edge slightly higher or lower. I will keep this band fixed for now so I can judge the later move fairly.”','selfCheck':'Point to earlier reactions, accept that a reasonable boundary can vary, and keep the chosen zone fixed during the first comparison.'},
 {'image':'charts/L13-02-return-toward-area.png','alt':'The same chart through 22 August 2014, showing the break and a later candle whose low approaches the zone edge.','title':'Stage 2 · Price returns towards the area','prompt':'Does the visible low enter the band, touch its edge, or stop short? Where does the candle close?','placeholder':'Describe the low and close separately; leave the outcome open for now.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “Price has moved above the old area and the later low approaches the band. I will describe the exact low relative to the drawn edge, then check the close separately. This is a return to assess; it does not yet prove that the area is acting as support.”','selfCheck':'Describe the low in relation to the displayed band, name the close location, and avoid declaring the retest successful before seeing the response.'},
 {'image':'charts/L13-03-later-evidence.png','alt':'The full selected NIFTY 50 window through 18 September 2014, with later price remaining above the candidate area.','title':'Stage 3 · Later evidence','prompt':'What does the later sequence support in this window? Which stronger claim would go too far?','placeholder':'Describe the response, then name one thing the chart still cannot promise.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “In this window, price remains above the candidate area after the return, which supports a hold-like reading. That does not prove the area will hold forever or that every breakout needs a retest. A later close back inside could change the picture.”','selfCheck':'Connect the return to the later response, keep the conclusion limited to this window, and mention what could change it.'}
]
by['13']['reveal']='In the selected window, price returns towards the candidate area and later remains above it. That supports a hold-like reading here. A different sequence could close back inside the old range or remain mixed; the area does not become permanent support just because it held once.'
by['13']['modelAnswerRubric']=['Names the original area and how price moved relative to it.','Separates the return (low or wick) from the candle close.','Describes the later response rather than guessing in advance.','Keeps the claim limited and names what could change it.']
by['13']['quiz']=[
 {'q':'Price breaks above a range and continues higher without returning. What can you conclude?','options':['No retest occurred in the window; one was not required.','The breakout is invalid because the zone was not tested.','A retest must happen before the next move.'],'correct':0,'feedback':'Some moves continue without returning. A retest is an additional event to assess, not a step every breakout must complete.'},
 {'q':'After an upside break, what does “role reversal” mean?','options':['The former resistance must hold on every visit.','The chart has confirmed a permanent support level.','The old resistance area may now act as support.'],'correct':2,'feedback':'Role reversal is a possibility to test. Price may hold above the area, cross back through it, or remain mixed.'},
 {'q':'The wick dips into the band, but the candle closes above it. What should you record?','options':['A failure is confirmed by the low alone.','The wick entered the area; the close remained above it.','The zone is no longer relevant.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'The low and close give different information. Record both, then look at the later response.'},
 {'q':'Three later candles close on alternating sides of the band. What is the fairest label?','options':['The role-reversal idea is confirmed.','The breakout must now reverse.','The response is still unclear.'],'correct':2,'feedback':'When candles keep crossing the band without moving away clearly, the answer is still unclear. You can leave it there for now.'},
 {'q':'Price closes inside the old range and a later reclaim attempt fails. What changed?','options':['The role-reversal idea has weakened.','The old zone must be redrawn around the newest candle.','A move to the opposite range edge is guaranteed.'],'correct':0,'feedback':'Closes in the old range and a failed reclaim are evidence against the idea that the old ceiling is holding as support.'},
 {'q':'You decide the band needs to be wider after seeing more candles. What is the best practice?','options':['Replace the old band without a note.','Keep both versions and record why you revised it.','Choose whichever version makes the outcome look clean.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'A revision can be reasonable. Keeping the old boundary and noting the reason helps you avoid fitting the drawing to the ending.'},
 {'q':'Which note best describes a return that holds for now?','options':['“The level worked; it is support from now on.”','“Buy the next touch of the area.”','“Price returned to the band and moved away while staying above it.”'],'correct':2,'feedback':'The third note describes the visible sequence and keeps the conclusion limited to this visit.'}
]

by['14']['stages']=[
 {'image':'charts/L14-01-before-break.png','alt':'NIFTY 50 daily candles through 19 January 2018, showing the candidate former-resistance band before the breakout.','title':'Stage 1 · Before the breakout attempt','prompt':'Which earlier reactions define your candidate area? What would make you question a future move above it?','placeholder':'State the prior evidence, the boundary you chose, and one possible warning sign.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “Earlier highs cluster near this band, so I will use it as the candidate ceiling. I will count a daily close above it as a breakout attempt. A close back inside the old range, especially if price cannot reclaim the band, would weaken the idea that the move is holding.”','selfCheck':'Use earlier candles to explain the zone, state what would count as a break, and describe a future contradiction without assuming it will happen.'},
 {'image':'charts/L14-02-initial-return.png','alt':'The same NIFTY 50 chart through 1 February 2018, showing a move above the band and a return that still closes above it.','title':'Stage 2 · The return still holds above','prompt':'Price has moved above the band and returned towards it, but the close remains above. What is plausible, and what is still missing?','placeholder':'Describe the current evidence and keep at least one alternative open.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “The close remains above the band after the return, so the idea that former resistance may be acting as support is still plausible. I do not yet have evidence of a sustained failure. Later closes back inside or a failed reclaim would change the reading.”','selfCheck':'Notice the close remains above, describe role reversal as possible rather than certain, and state what evidence is still missing.'},
 {'image':'charts/L14-03-failed-follow-through.png','alt':'The same NIFTY 50 series through 20 February 2018, showing the close below the band and later weakness.','title':'Stage 3 · The continuation idea is challenged','prompt':'Which new evidence weakens the idea that price will keep holding above the zone? What does it not prove?','placeholder':'Name the close or later response that changed your view; avoid inventing a cause or an automatic trade.','modelAnswer':'You could write: “The 2 February close back below the original band contradicts the idea that price was holding above it. Later weakness adds context. The first return had looked acceptable, but the new close changes the reading. It does not prove who caused the failure or mean a short trade is automatic.”','selfCheck':'Point to the close back below the band, explain how it weakens the continuation idea, and leave motive and trade choice unclaimed.'}
]
by['14']['reveal']='The return on 1 February still closed above the band, so a hold-like reading remained plausible at that stage. The 2 February close back below the band then challenged that idea, and later weakness added context. The first view was not foolish; the evidence changed. This does not prove hidden intent or prescribe a short trade.'
by['14']['modelAnswerRubric']=['Names the boundary and the original breakout convention.','Points to the new candle or sequence that changed the reading.','Explains what the evidence weakens without claiming certainty.','Does not infer hidden motive or turn the failure into an automatic trade.']
by['14']['quiz']=[
 {'q':'A candle has just closed above a range ceiling. How should you label it at this point?','options':['A confirmed move that must continue higher.','A false breakout, even before a return.','A breakout attempt; the next response is still unknown.'],'correct':2,'feedback':'At the first close you can record the attempt. A failure label needs later evidence that challenges the move.'},
 {'q':'One candle then closes back inside the old range. What does that tell you?','options':['The next move lower is certain.','The continuation idea has been challenged.','The original zone was definitely wrong.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'A close back inside is a clear contradiction to the idea that price is holding outside, but one close does not guarantee the next direction.'},
 {'q':'After a failed upside break, which paths may still happen?','options':['Price may move into the range, chop, or reclaim the zone.','Price must fall to the opposite range edge.','Only a direct trend reversal remains possible.'],'correct':0,'feedback':'Failure makes alternatives worth reviewing. It does not select a single outcome.'},
 {'q':'For a hypothetical short after an upside failure, which price point matters structurally?','options':['Any fixed number of points above the old zone.','The exact midpoint of the prior range.','The failure extreme that would challenge the failure idea.'],'correct':2,'feedback':'The structural reference should relate to the move that failed. It is not a universal buffer or an entry instruction; if the risk is too large, passing may be appropriate.'},
 {'q':'A market gaps from above a zone to below it. What can the candles tell you?','options':['Every traded price inside the gap.','The shown open and range, but not the missing path.','That price smoothly crossed every point.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'The chart does not show trades inside the gap. Describe the prices the data supplies and leave the missing path unknown.'},
 {'q':'High volume appears during a failed breakout. What does that prove?','options':['Nothing about who traded or why, by itself.','That stop orders were deliberately targeted.','That the next move must reverse strongly.'],'correct':0,'feedback':'Volume can describe reported activity, but it does not establish hidden motive or guarantee follow-through.'},
 {'q':'Price returns into the range, then later reclaims the ceiling and stays above. What should the review say?','options':['The earlier return should be deleted from the notes.','The first failure guarantees the next attempt.','The reclaim is new evidence against the failure reading.'],'correct':2,'feedback':'Both events matter. Keep the earlier return, then note that the later reclaim challenges your current label.'},
 {'q':'A neckline break fails and later candles cross the neckline repeatedly. Which description fits best?','options':['The named pattern guarantees the opposite move.','The break has not established itself; the result is still unclear.','The first crossing proves the pattern has completed.'],'correct':1,'feedback':'A pattern name does not settle the result. Repeated overlap can leave the interpretation unresolved.'}
]

